\A{feedback} \ii{Feedback} and \i{bug reporting}

This appendix covers bug reports, security vulnerabilities, feature
requests and code contributions for KiTTY++.

KiTTY++ is a Windows-only fork of PuTTY and KiTTY, maintained by
kapper.net (KAPPER NETWORK-COMMUNICATIONS GmbH). Report problems with
KiTTY++ to the KiTTY++ project, not to the PuTTY team.

\H{feedback-where} Where to report

Report bugs and feature requests as issues in the KiTTY++ issue
tracker on GitHub:

\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/issues}\cw{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/issues}

The issue tracker is public. Security vulnerabilities do not belong
there: see \k{feedback-vulns}.

\H{feedback-before} Before you report

\b Search the
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/issues}{existing issues} for the
same problem. If an issue already exists, add your details to it
instead of opening a new one.

\b Read the CHANGELOG of the newest release on the
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/releases}{releases page}. The
problem may already be fixed.

\H{feedback-bugs} Reporting bugs

A useful bug report contains:

\b The \i{version of KiTTY++}. The About box shows it (\q{About
KiTTY++} in the System menu).

\b The Windows version.

\b Whether you run the 32-bit or the 64-bit build.

\b Whether KiTTY++ is installed (MSI) or portable (ZIP).

\b The exact steps that reproduce the problem.

\b What happened and what you expected to happen instead.

\b The Event Log lines around the problem. Choose \q{Event Log} from
the System menu, select the lines and click Copy (see
\k{using-eventlog}).

\b For a connection problem: the server software and its version.

Never put passwords, passphrases or private keys in a report. Event
Log lines can contain host names, user names and IP addresses. Mask
them if needed.

\H{feedback-vulns} Reporting security vulnerabilities

Do not report a security vulnerability as a public issue. Report it
privately through GitHub's private vulnerability reporting: open the
Security tab of the repository at
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/security}\cw{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/security}
and click \q{Report a vulnerability}.

Include the same details as in a bug report (\k{feedback-bugs}).

\H{feedback-features} Requesting features

Open an issue for a feature request. Describe the use case: what you
want to achieve and why the current features do not cover it.

\H{feedback-patches} Contributing code

Code contributions are welcome as pull requests on GitHub at
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY}\cw{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY}.
